#
# Linux for the Kria KR260's processing system, beside the CADR in its fabric.
#
#     make buildroot-kr260    # from the repository root; see the Makefile
#
# What comes out (output/images/): boot.scr, the script the factory U-Boot
# runs from the card; Image; zynqmp-smk-k26-revA-sck-kr-g-revB-cadr.dtb, the
# CADR's tree; and rootfs.cpio.uboot.  No loader: the board's boot firmware
# is the one AMD ships in its QSPI flash, and nothing here builds or writes
# it.
#
# **IT NEEDS BOTH EXTERNAL TREES**, because the packages live in the Arty
# Z7-20's and this one holds only the board, as the Cora Z7-07S's and the
# DE25-Nano's do:
#
#     BR2_EXTERNAL=boards/arty-z7-20/linux/buildroot:boards/kria-kr260/linux/buildroot
#
# `$(BR2_EXTERNAL_CADR_PATH)` below is that first tree and
# `$(BR2_EXTERNAL_CADR_KR260_PATH)` this one.
#
# THE BOOT CHAIN IS THE FACTORY'S.  At power-on the QSPI flash's FSBL, PMU
# firmware, TF-A and U-Boot 2023.01 run (AMD's 2023.2 Kria boot firmware), and
# U-Boot's distro boot finds boot.scr at the root of the card, which the board
# reaches as a USB disk.  boot.scr (board/kria-kr260/boot.cmd) loads the tree,
# the kernel, the root filesystem and then the fabric, and boots.  The PMU
# firmware and TF-A stay resident, and the kernel asks them for clocks, power
# and resets through the ZynqMP firmware interface.
#
# EVERYTHING IS PINNED, as on the Arty Z7-20: Buildroot by version and sha256
# in the Makefile, and through it the toolchain, the kernel (Buildroot's
# latest mainline, the Arty Z7-20's) and every package by the hashes
# Buildroot carries.  Nothing is fetched by commit.

BR2_aarch64=y
BR2_cortex_a53=y
BR2_DL_DIR="$(BR2_EXTERNAL_CADR_PATH)/../../../../vendor/buildroot-dl"
BR2_DOWNLOAD_FORCE_CHECK_HASHES=y

# The same prebuilt toolchain as the DE25-Nano's: aarch64, glibc.
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL=y
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_BOOTLIN=y
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_BOOTLIN_AARCH64_GLIBC_STABLE=y

# The board on a private LAN, as the others are.  The console is UART 1,
# ttyPS1 to Linux: `serial1 = &uart1` and `stdout-path = "serial1:115200n8"`
# in mainline's SOM tree.  The network port is the J10C jack, GEM1, which the
# kernel names eth1: both of the processing system's Ethernet controllers are
# enabled in the carrier's tree, GEM0 at 0xFF0B_0000 on J10D first, and the
# kernel names them in that order.
BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_HOSTNAME="cadr"
BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_ISSUE="the Kria KR260 beside the CADR"
BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_ROOT_PASSWD="root"
BR2_SYSTEM_DHCP="eth1"
BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_GETTY_PORT="ttyPS1"
BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_GETTY_BAUDRATE_115200=y

# The kernel: mainline, Buildroot's latest, the version the Arty Z7-20 runs,
# because mainline has every driver this board needs and the K26 SOM's and
# the KR260 carrier's trees.  Mainline's arm64 `defconfig` and our fragment
# over it; the fragment's header says what it changes and why.  The tree is
# mainline's own KR260 tree, which the kernel builds by applying the carrier's
# overlay to the SOM's; post-image.sh applies the CADR's additions to it.
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL=y
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_USE_ARCH_DEFAULT_CONFIG=y
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CONFIG_FRAGMENT_FILES="$(BR2_EXTERNAL_CADR_KR260_PATH)/board/kria-kr260/linux/linux.fragment"
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_IMAGE=y
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DTS_SUPPORT=y
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_INTREE_DTS_NAME="xilinx/zynqmp-smk-k26-revA-sck-kr-g-revB"

# The root filesystem: BusyBox, Dropbear, evtest for the USB keyboard and
# mouse, and every program of ours.  NOTHING IS LEFT OUT: the board has four
# USB host ports, so the USB input program is in, as on the Arty Z7-20.  An
# initramfs as a U-Boot ramdisk image, as on the other boards.
BR2_PACKAGE_DROPBEAR=y
# BusyBox's ntpd, for the clock by NTP at boot (S80cadr-disk-packs).  The
# fragment is shared by the four boards and says why each applet is there.
BR2_PACKAGE_BUSYBOX_CONFIG_FRAGMENT_FILES="$(BR2_EXTERNAL_CADR_PATH)/board/arty-z7-20/busybox.fragment"
BR2_PACKAGE_EVTEST=y
BR2_PACKAGE_CADR_CONSOLE=y
BR2_PACKAGE_CADR_READOUT=y
BR2_PACKAGE_CADR_CHECKPOINT=y
BR2_PACKAGE_CADR_DISK_PACKS=y
BR2_PACKAGE_CADR_TERMINAL=y
BR2_PACKAGE_CADR_SERIAL=y
BR2_PACKAGE_CADR_CHAOSNET=y
BR2_PACKAGE_CADR_USB_INPUT=y
BR2_PACKAGE_QUUX_FILE_DEVICE=y
BR2_PACKAGE_MUIR=y
# ozd, the band's file and time host, on the board itself, as on every board.
BR2_PACKAGE_OZD=y
BR2_ROOTFS_USERS_TABLES="$(BR2_EXTERNAL_CADR_PATH)/package/ozd/users.table"
# **THE PROGRAMS ARE BUILT WITH THIS BOARD'S ADDRESS MAP.**  The faces behind
# M_AXI_HPM0_FPD at 0xA000_0000, the console behind M_AXI_HPM1_FPD at
# 0xB000_0000, the machine's memory reserved at 0x6300_0000 and the tally on
# EMIO; package/cadr-common/src/cadr/cadr_board.h has the table and its
# sources.
BR2_CADR_BOARD_KR260=y
# The card is a USB disk on this board, so it is found by the carrier's own
# reader, USB 0424:2240, and not as the processor's first SD device; the
# overlay's /etc/cadr/card.sh says how, and S80cadr-disk-packs reads it.
BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY="$(BR2_EXTERNAL_CADR_KR260_PATH)/board/kria-kr260/rootfs-overlay"
# The Arty Z7-20's post-build script, unchanged, for the reason the Cora
# Z7-07S's defconfig gives: it asserts that the image holds only the programs
# the packages install.  Then this board's own, which takes the kernel's
# loadable modules back out of the image.
BR2_ROOTFS_POST_BUILD_SCRIPT="$(BR2_EXTERNAL_CADR_PATH)/board/arty-z7-20/post-build.sh $(BR2_EXTERNAL_CADR_KR260_PATH)/board/kria-kr260/post-build.sh"
# And after the images: the CADR's tree and boot.scr.
BR2_ROOTFS_POST_IMAGE_SCRIPT="$(BR2_EXTERNAL_CADR_KR260_PATH)/board/kria-kr260/post-image.sh"
BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_CPIO=y
BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_CPIO_GZIP=y
BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_CPIO_UIMAGE=y
# BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_TAR is not set

# The host tools post-image.sh and the card staging use: mkimage for boot.scr
# and the ramdisk image, and dtc's fdtoverlay and fdtget for the tree.
BR2_PACKAGE_HOST_DTC=y
BR2_PACKAGE_HOST_UBOOT_TOOLS=y
